@@ -0,0 +1,163 @@
+From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
+From: Dom Cobley <popcornmix@gmail.com>
+Date: Wed, 12 Aug 2026 21:41:09 +0100
+Subject: [PATCH 18/46] DVDDemuxFFmpeg: detect the second view of an H.264 MVC
+ stream
+
+MV-HEVC announces itself in the container, so the multiview machinery could key
+off the AVStereo3D side data the demuxer already exports. H.264 MVC announces
+nothing: the base view SPS is plain High profile, matroska carries no StereoMode
+and no stereo3d side data, and the decoder's view_ids_available list stays empty
+until the subset SPS turns up in band - two packets into the stream on the
+sample tested.
+
+That is too late. Requesting both views has to happen before avcodec_open2(),
+because it also decides whether a hwaccel may attach, and no hwaccel decodes the
+dependent layer. Waiting for the decoder to notice would mean either giving up
+hardware decoding for every H.264 stream, which is not a trade worth making on a
+Pi, or reopening the codec mid-stream.
+
+There is a container level signal, just not one ffmpeg surfaces: the MVC
+configuration is appended to the base avcC as an ISO 14496-15 mvcC box holding
+the subset SPS. Find it by box type and validate the size that precedes it,
+rather than walking the avcC, whose trailing high profile fields are optional
+and leave its end ambiguous. Both Blu-ray rips tested carry a well formed box
+declaring Stereo High.
+
+The stream is only marked multiview when the decoder can decode the second
+view, which is whether its option table carries view_ids - a runtime check,
+because H.264 gained the option well after HEVC and a version cannot separate
+the two. Marking it regardless would cost the stream its hardware decoder,
+since multiview forces software decoding before the option can be refused, and
+get nothing back: an ffmpeg without H.264 MVC support therefore keeps hardware
+decoding the base view exactly as it does today, and the stream stays 2D. The
+refusal path in the decoders remains as the backstop.
+
+The base view is the left eye. Nothing in these streams says otherwise - the
+tested samples export no view_pos_available - but the decoder already corrects
+the reported mode when a bitstream does say which view is which.
+
+Everything after detection is the machinery MV-HEVC needed: the h264 decoder
+takes the same view_ids option, exports the same view_ids_available, and tags
+frames with the same AV_FRAME_DATA_VIEW_ID, so the views are packed by the same
+filter graph. One difference is worth noting: the frame before the subset SPS
+appears has no view id at all, and it is the defensive path that feeds such a
+frame to both graph inputs which keeps that first frame from stalling the graph
+or offsetting the pairing for the rest of the stream.
+
+Verified on a Pi 4 against a Blu-ray MVC rip: the box is found and both views
+are decoded and packed.
+
+(cherry picked from commit f8c216ab0709bb4b760e752f1cf2deb89dff978d)
+---
+ .../DVDDemuxers/DVDDemuxFFmpeg.cpp            | 64 +++++++++++++++++++
+ .../VideoPlayer/DVDDemuxers/DVDDemuxFFmpeg.h  |  7 ++
+ 2 files changed, 71 insertions(+)
+
