diff --git a/projects/RPi/devices/RPi5/patches/kodi/0022-VideoPlayer-play-the-second-view-of-a-3D-Blu-ray.patch b/projects/RPi/devices/RPi5/patches/kodi/0022-VideoPlayer-play-the-second-view-of-a-3D-Blu-ray.patch
new file mode 100644
index 0000000000..2710e858cc
--- /dev/null
+++ b/projects/RPi/devices/RPi5/patches/kodi/0022-VideoPlayer-play-the-second-view-of-a-3D-Blu-ray.patch
@@ -0,0 +1,1860 @@
+From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
+From: Dom Cobley <popcornmix@gmail.com>
+Date: Wed, 12 Aug 2026 21:41:57 +0100
+Subject: [PATCH 22/46] VideoPlayer: play the second view of a 3D Blu-ray
+
+A 3D Blu-ray codes its two eyes as two clips: the base view that libbluray
+plays and a 2D player sees, and the MVC dependent view in a clip of its own,
+named by the stereoscopic sub-path of the playlist. Nothing opened it, so a
+3D disc or ISO played in 2D however capable the decoder. The mvcC-based
+detection cannot fire either, as a transport stream carries no extradata.
+
+libbluray parses the 3D metadata but wires none of it to playback, and has
+no demuxer to merge the views with, so do it here with what it already
+offers. CDVDDemuxBluray3D runs a second CDVDDemuxFFmpeg over the dependent
+clip and appends each of its access units to the base view packet with the
+same timestamp, growing that packet in place; the stream list, chapters,
+seeking and programs stay the base demuxer's. The clip is opened through
+bd_open_file_dec(), so AACS and BD+ are handled and an image, a folder and a
+disc behave alike. CDVDInputStreamBlurayFile wraps that handle: it reads and
+seeks in the 6144 byte aligned units the descrambler accepts, and buffers
+about 3 MiB so the two clips, which sit far apart on the disc, do not seek
+against each other. The video stream is marked multiview with the eye order
+the playlist's MVC_Base_view_R_flag gives, which is logged, and from there on
+it is the machinery MV-HEVC and matroska MVC already use.
+
+The stereoscopic sub-path lives in playlist extension data libbluray keeps
+private, so CDVDInputStreamBluray re-reads the playlist with Kodi's MPLS
+parser, through a udf:// root for a disc image.
+
+Pairing the views:
+
+- The dependent view is demuxed unparsed (AVFMT_FLAG_NOPARSE) and framed
+  by the container, one access unit per PES packet: its slices are NAL 20,
+  which the H.264 parser never treats as a picture start. The mpegts
+  demuxer is told to emit a packet as large as an access unit, since it
+  otherwise hands a PES payload over in pieces of 200 KiB with only the
+  first timestamped, and a dependent view I-frame can be several times
+  that; a piece would be dropped as unpairable and the frame decoded
+  truncated. It is not probed either: its parameters cannot be found, and
+  the probe's extradata extractor gives up on a start-code-less piece, so
+  the streams are taken from the program map.
+
+- Each demuxer rebases timestamps on its own stream start time, so the
+  offset between them is computed from the two format contexts rather
+  than measured from a packet pair - after a seek there is no known good
+  pair. Catching the dependent view up is bounded to 10 s, because
+  ConvertTimestamp() hands the first packet of a clip over without
+  subtracting the start time.
+
+- The dependent view is placed 3 s early, so that it is behind the base view
+  and can catch up. On opening a clip that is the in time the playlist gives
+  its stereoscopic sub play item; after a seek it is the first base view
+  timestamp that arrives, the time the player seeks to being on the title's
+  timeline while a clip's timestamps are its own. Neither will do for the
+  other. The base view cannot place a clip being opened: libbluray reaches
+  the next play item while the base view access units of the one before it
+  are still coming out of the demuxer, so the next base view timestamp may
+  still belong to the play item being left, and where each clip restarts its
+  timestamps seeking to it lands the new clip past its own end. The in time
+  cannot place a seek, the play item it lands in needing not be entered at
+  its start. A run of 12 unpaired base access units places the dependent
+  view again, for clips joined without continuous timestamps - at most once
+  every 5 s, and not once the clip has simply run out.
+
+Following the disc:
+
+- Whether a title is 3D cannot be settled when the demuxer is made: in
+  navigation mode libbluray chooses the playlist while reading for the
+  demuxer that has just been created. So this demuxer is used for any
+  disc whose application info declares 3D content, and the dependent view
+  comes and goes with the play item: the clip the playlist names for the
+  current play item is checked as each base view access unit goes by,
+  since a play item change does not report a stream change and a feature
+  is routinely spread over dozens of them. A stream change rebuilds the
+  stream list, so the base view is found in it again and the multiview
+  flag put back. The dependent view's absence is an ordinary state - the
+  2D parts of a 3D disc play through this demuxer with nothing appended -
+  and only a play item with a second eye claims to be multiview, which
+  matters because claiming it costs the stream its hardware decoder.
+
+- A clip that fails to open (some menu play items name a dependent view
+  with no video stream) is remembered and not retried until the play item
+  names another or a flush gives it another chance.
+
+- Reset() lets the dependent view go and leaves the next base view access
+  unit to open the clip its play item names afresh. A dependent view
+  demuxer cannot be reset in place: that re-probes from wherever the input
+  was left, fails, and leaves an unopened format context that crashes on
+  the next seek.
+
+- Abort() arrives from another thread while the demux thread may be
+  replacing the dependent view demuxer, so that pointer is guarded, and
+  nothing is opened once an abort has been asked for.
+
+An ffmpeg without H.264 MVC support (no view_ids option) and a
+stereoscopic playback mode of mono both leave the title to the ordinary 2D
+path, as does a base view that fails to open.
+
+[Bryan Hoban: EnableStream returns bool in this Kodi]
