@@ -0,0 +1,552 @@
+From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
+From: Dom Cobley <popcornmix@gmail.com>
+Date: Sat, 29 Aug 2026 19:43:40 +0100
+Subject: [PATCH 26/46] VideoPlayer: put Blu-ray 3D subtitles at the depth the
+ disc asks for
+
+A Blu-ray 3D subtitle plane is monoscopic, and its depth is a horizontal shift the disc
+varies frame by frame - one way for the left eye, the other for the right. Nothing read
+that, so the same bitmap went to both eyes at the same place and the subtitles sat at
+screen depth for the length of the film, wherever the scene in front of them was. All of
+the depth an author put in was discarded in silence.
+
+The metadata and the sequence a subtitle stream follows are now read; this carries them
+to the overlay. The demuxer already pairs each dependent view access unit with its base
+view partner, so the offsets come out of the dependent one and are stored against the
+base view's timestamp, which is the time the frame they describe will be shown.
+CDVDInputStreamBluray owns the store and the playlist that says which sequence each
+subtitle PID follows, being the one object that outlives both the demuxer and any single
+play item; the player takes a reference to it when the stream opens and remembers the
+sequence of the subtitle it is showing.
+
+Those are the demuxer's timestamps and the ones the player presents are not: ReadPacket()
+takes m_offset_pts off every packet to keep the clock monotonic where a clip restarts its
+timestamps, which on a feature spread over several play items is most of the film. A
+lookup therefore puts it back, or it would find nothing from the first clip change on and
+the subtitles would sit at screen depth for the rest of the film. m_offset_pts is read on
+the render thread for that, so it is atomic.
+
+The render thread asks for the frame it is presenting, through IRenderMsg, because the
+value belongs to a frame rather than to a subtitle - the plane moves while a line of
+dialogue stays on screen. The default implementation answers nothing at all, so only a
+Blu-ray 3D title with a subtitle stream that follows a sequence sees any of this:
+external subtitles, a 2D title and a disc that says nothing all take the same path they
+do today.
+
+Applied where the subtitles.stereoscopicdepth setting already shifts an overlay, and in
+its place rather than beside it. A title placing its own subtitles in depth is answering
+the question that setting exists to answer, and answering it better - frame by frame,
+from what the author put there - so a viewer who has set a depth for the files that carry
+none should not have it added to a disc that does. An offset of 0 from such a title is
+one of its answers, the subtitle belonging at the screen for that frame, rather than an
+absence for the setting to fill. The disc's offset is in the video's own pixels and the
+plane is drawn over the video, so it is scaled by the same ratio the overlay is, and it
+is signed by the eye being drawn either way.
+
+The offsets describe a run of frames from where they were read, so they are dropped
+whenever that ceases to be true: on a seek, when the play item changes, and when the
+playlist is re-read.
+
+Measured on a retail disc: with the disc calling for 7, 8, 9, 10 and 13 pixels at
+various points, each subtitle lands that many pixels from the position its own bitmap
+declares, and moves when the disc says it should.
+
