+From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
+From: Dom Cobley <popcornmix@gmail.com>
+Date: Sun, 30 Aug 2026 11:59:04 +0100
+Subject: [PATCH 27/46] Stereoscopics: settle the display mode for a 3D source
+ in one change
+
+Starting a stereoscopic title reconfigured the display three times in the six
+seconds before the film appeared, and on a projector each of those is a
+resynchronisation - the last of them, into a 3D mode, several seconds of it.
+
+The first came from the search that runs when the video stream opens. The
+stereo mode that decides what the display mode has to carry is applied when the
+first picture reaches the renderer, which on a 4K file is seconds later, so the
+search ran while the output was still 2D and picked the 2D mode of the right
+timing - a mode chosen for a frame nobody was going to see, since nothing of the
+film is on screen until the picture that settles the question arrives. Leave the
+display alone until then. Only where a stereo mode is certain to be applied
+without asking: with the playback mode set to ask the viewer's answer may be a
+while coming, and with it set to ignore no mode is coming at all, so in both the
+search goes ahead as before.
+
+The second and third were the stereo mode and the display mode arriving
+separately. Applying a stereo mode re-applies the display mode by itself - the
+surface changes shape with it - so the mode change that follows a moment later,
+when the player's search has run, reconfigures a display that was reconfigured
+for the same reason a frame earlier. So when CGraphicContext::Flip() applies a
+pending stereo mode it asks the stereoscopics manager for the display mode the
+video being played needs in that arrangement, and applies the two together. That
+keeps the whole decision on the render thread: the stereo mode is requested from
+the player's event thread, so the request itself is atomic, and nothing else
+crosses between the two. The player is still asked to run its search afterwards,
+which is what keeps the display latency and its own parameters in step; by then
+the mode is already set, so it settles on the same one and changes nothing.
+
+ChooseBestResolution() therefore takes the arrangement to choose for, defaulting
+to the one the graphics context has been asked for, so that Flip() can choose
+for the mode it is in the middle of applying.
+
+Which mode a stereoscopic source's search is waiting for is the manager's to
+say, so it says it - IsStereoModeSettled(), true once it has decided for a
+stereoscopic stream, and atomic since the render thread reads it. The decision
+is announced as a stream change like any other, so where it changed nothing the
+search that was deferred still has to be asked for; that ask is made once, since
+servicing it is announced as a stream change in turn and asking again on that
+would not end. A new item starting has nothing settled about it, so the flag is
+cleared then as well as on stop: playback running on from one item to the next
+never stops, and a 3D title after a 2D one would otherwise have its display mode
+chosen before its stereo mode.
+
+Finally, compare the mode in effect against the arrangement the display can
+carry rather than the one the source asks for. They differ whenever the display
+carries only the other arrangement, and a source left in its own - which is what
+happens when the geometry needed to choose is not known yet - would otherwise
+never be revisited.
+
