diff --git a/projects/RPi/devices/RPi5/patches/kodi/0030-Bluray-do-not-mistake-one-3D-presentation-of-a-featu.patch b/projects/RPi/devices/RPi5/patches/kodi/0030-Bluray-do-not-mistake-one-3D-presentation-of-a-featu.patch
new file mode 100644
index 0000000000..b42688cc88
--- /dev/null
+++ b/projects/RPi/devices/RPi5/patches/kodi/0030-Bluray-do-not-mistake-one-3D-presentation-of-a-featu.patch
@@ -0,0 +1,564 @@
+From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
+From: Dom Cobley <popcornmix@gmail.com>
+Date: Thu, 3 Sep 2026 16:07:13 +0100
+Subject: [PATCH 30/46] Bluray: do not mistake one 3D presentation of a feature
+ for another
+
+A 3D Blu-ray carries both presentations of its feature, and they are alike in everything the
+duplicate searches compare: same length, same chapters, same clip for the base view, and the
+same audio and subtitle streams exposed. What tells them apart is in the playlist's extension
+data, which PlaylistInformation did not carry at all - so the two collapsed to one entry, and
+as the copy dropped was the higher numbered of the pair, and the 3D playlist is usually
+numbered above its 2D twin, what a viewer was left with was the 2D one. The disc then plays
+flat with no sign of a second eye anywhere, and no way to ask for one.
+
+Two things are carried through and compared now: the clips holding the dependent view, and
+which of the two coded views the playlist calls the left eye (MVC_Base_view_R_flag).
+Presentations disagreeing about either are two presentations. A disc can hold the same
+feature over the same clips both ways round - Drive Angry has playlists 99 and 100, alike in
+everything else, and only 100 has the eyes the way its author intended; the disc's own
+navigation plays that one, while the playlist search kept the lower number and left a viewer
+with a film inside out and no way to reach the other. The flag says nothing where a playlist
+names no second eye, so it is only read alongside one, and copies of a 2D feature go on
+collapsing however it happens to be set.
+
+Where only one of a pair is stereoscopic, that is the one kept: its base view is the 2D
+presentation, so a viewer who wants 2D loses nothing (the stereoscopic playback mode setting
+already plays the base view alone), while dropping it costs the disc its 3D. Both the
+directory listing and the movie search do this now - the listing alone did, and the movie
+search is what answers the playlist chooser and the library, which is where a viewer meets
+the feature.
+
+Duplicate removal is also logged now, saying which playlist a discarded one duplicates.
+FilterPlaylists() logs everything else it drops, on the grounds that a playlist removed
+there is otherwise indistinguishable from one that is not on the disc, and this one was the
+exception - which is why two reports of a 3D disc playing flat had nothing in the log to
+explain it, only a count of playlists read that did not add up to what was offered.
+
+Covered by tests: the stereoscopic copy is kept whichever way round the disc numbers the
+pair, and among several 2D copies; two stereoscopic presentations of the same content both
+survive, whether they differ by dependent view clip or by base view eye; the base view eye of
+a playlist naming no second eye is ignored; and two copies of the same stereoscopic
+presentation still collapse to the lowest numbered.
+
+(cherry picked from commit 258b5f45f499885de21ad9e1e8be37190b99aa5a)
