+A disc played as one of its playlists has no way back to the disc's own menu.
+The OSD offers nothing, because a menu is only offered while one is being
+navigated, and the choice before playback is gone once a playlist is known: a
+library entry holds the playlist the scan settled on, so automatic menu
+selection sees a playlist and does not ask. The disc's menu is then unreachable
+for as long as the entry lives, which is what people notice.
+
+Say separately from the menu being navigated that one could be, and let the
+player act on it. Not the menu type: that describes what is being navigated now,
+and whether it is NONE decides how streams are opened and how the demuxer is
+synchronised, so widening it would change how every playlist plays.
+
+Asking libbluray for a menu while it is playing a playlist gets nowhere - it
+never entered navigation mode - so play the disc again as a menu path, which is
+what choosing the menu beforehand would have done. Two things in the way of that
+trip, both of which decide the item is still the playlist it came from:
+
+The path is settled again on the way through, and an item whose tag names a
+bluray:// path has the tag's path put back over it. For a library entry that is
+the playlist, so the menu has to be written to the tag as well or it does not
+survive.
+
+Then what to play is decided again, and a playlist search would be run for the
+menu - answering nothing, and taking a "simple menu" or "main title" setting
+straight back off the menu. A path that is already a menu has had that decision
+made, so leave it alone. This also makes the existing route to the menu
+idempotent, which it was not.
+
+Seen on a 3D disc whose menu the disc's own navigator reaches: the action now
+opens the menu path, and the disc navigates to its top title. What the menu then
+draws is the disc's business - a BD-J one still needs a JVM libbluray can load.
+
