+From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
+From: Dom Cobley <popcornmix@gmail.com>
+Date: Sat, 5 Sep 2026 15:37:45 +0100
+Subject: [PATCH 34/46] VideoPicture: carry the two views of a stereoscopic
+ picture separately
+
+A picture holds one buffer, so a decoder with an eye in each hand has nowhere to
+put the second one. That is why the multiview decoders pack the views side by
+side: not because anything wants a packed frame, but because it is the only shape
+that fits through here. The renderers then undo it, cropping an eye back out.
+
+Give a picture a second buffer and a flag saying the views arrived that way. The
+flag sits beside the pointer rather than being inferred from it because
+SetParams() drops the buffer references, and the copy of the picture that a
+CVideoBufferDRMPRIME caches is the only thing a renderer has to go on when it is
+deciding, before it has ever been configured, whether it can present the stream
+at all.
+
+CONF_FLAGS_STEREO_MODE_SEPARATE takes the free value in the existing mode mask
+and says the frame is not packed. It is worth having as its own value rather than
+as a flag beside SBS: ManageRenderArea() then falls through its switch and leaves
+the source rectangle alone, and GetPerEyeAspectRatio() falls through to the plain
+ratio, which is right, because a view that was never packed already has the aspect
+it should. So the shared geometry needs no teaching. The cadence bit is untouched
+and keeps saying which eye leads, so right_left sources and the viewer's eye swap
+go on working as they are.
+
+GetEffectiveStereoView() is the swap that ManageRenderArea() already did inline,
+lifted out so a renderer choosing between two buffers and one cropping a packed
+frame agree on which eye they are drawing.
+
+Nothing produces a second buffer yet.
+
