index 0000000000..038b781416
--- /dev/null
+++ b/projects/RPi/devices/RPi5/patches/kodi/0038-DVDVideoCodecDRMPRIME-hand-the-two-views-over-unpack.patch
@@ -0,0 +1,759 @@
+From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
+From: Dom Cobley <popcornmix@gmail.com>
+Date: Sat, 5 Sep 2026 15:38:29 +0100
+Subject: [PATCH 38/46] DVDVideoCodecDRMPRIME: hand the two views over unpacked
+
+Now that a picture can carry both views and the DRM PRIME renderers can present
+them, there is no reason to pack them first. The decoder's own frames go straight
+to the renderer, so hstack's copy - a whole frame read and a double-width frame
+written, per picture - goes away, and with it the pool of double-width buffers it
+wrote into and the frame of latency its graph added. An unfiltered 3D stream now
+runs with no filter graph at all.
+
+CMultiviewFramePairer keeps the part that was always about pairing - holding a base
+view frame back until the frame after it says whether a dependent view is coming,
+settling the eye order from the bitstream, and turning an orphan into its own
+partner - and hands the pair to the caller instead of pushing it into two buffer
+sources. Feeding a packing graph becomes FeedMultiviewPair() beside it, so the two
+codecs still share it rather than growing a copy each.
+
+Packing stays for a stream that needs a filter chain, because the chain is one
+graph and running the views through it separately would need two. That is not the
+common case - MVC and MV-HEVC are progressive, so nothing asks for a deinterlacer -
+but a stream the decoder does not put out in a format we can hand over needs the
+scale filter, and 10 bit is such a format.
+
+Measured on a Pi 4. Where the decoder was keeping up, playing a 1080p MVC Blu-ray
+costs 163% of a core against 179% packed, 9% less. Where it was not, the packed
+path was quietly making up the difference by dropping: on Coraline it discards
+28% of pictures against 20%, and it needs forty seconds to recover from a seek,
+running thousands of frames late and repeating one about 380 times a second, where
+the unpacked path never falls behind at all.
+
+The dependent view is still decoded in software, so this does not make a 3D disc
+cheap - it stops it being more expensive than it has to be.
+
+[Bryan Hoban: attach the Dolby Vision enhancement layer to the base view]
