+From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
+From: Dom Cobley <popcornmix@gmail.com>
+Date: Sun, 13 Sep 2026 12:32:03 +0100
+Subject: [PATCH 40/46] VideoPlayer: do not end a title on a seek that went
+ nowhere
+
+Seek into some Blu-rays and the film stops as though it had reached the end.
+John Carter does it half an hour in; a remux of the same disc, seeked to the
+same place, does not.
+
+The seek is reported as having worked. libbluray's clip seek fails -- the file
+seek inside it returns an error -- but it only warns, keeps the position it had
+calculated and returns that, so bd_seek_time() succeeds and every layer above
+believes it. The flush that goes with the seek leaves nothing behind, the first
+read after it says end of file, and Process() has no way to tell that from a
+stream which has genuinely run out. So it ends the title.
+
+Give it a way to tell. A seek is always followed by a flush, so a read that
+fails before a single packet has arrived is the seek's doing rather than the
+stream's. Remember where playback was when the seek was asked for and go back
+there instead of ending. Once only: if there is nothing to be read there
+either, the title ends as it did before.
+
+A seek to the very end of a title now returns to where it came from, on the
+rare occasion that it lands with nothing at all left to read.
+
